#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0077e2b0-9780-54fe-afa4-b670dc9fed69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319393} {:answer/id #uuid "3acae8da-de1f-55f4-a6ed-13701c01de6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319570} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0903cd-0495-574d-b41e-abbe3013ddf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319571} {:answer/id #uuid "87860f14-7358-57f6-a4a5-2a37b1f9c9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319798} {:answer/id #uuid "cf85f8ab-e663-568c-9644-773ac18a9dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87860f14-7358-57f6-a4a5-2a37b1f9c9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319798} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4b9fed-d8b4-5a97-89f5-38bb7b5b308d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2304859f-a364-5093-b7c5-56d02eda6877" :question/math-academy-id "q-35298" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3095f8-ebd6-5417-b5fa-228deb53654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345465} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffb8a8a-2e10-5113-8365-30ccd72226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345633} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9aee49-f085-5882-8939-280b36b2183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} {:answer/id #uuid "9125c680-3dee-5b40-9892-fbcf815bf7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346617} {:answer/id #uuid "f560de8e-3259-522f-ba63-0c6f8892c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a9aee49-f085-5882-8939-280b36b2183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce3662f-50d2-5467-a302-1a748f1ec224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4dbe4152-1f13-5417-b4be-c6eaa0530f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79313" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]})=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f09dffb-bf3a-5fbb-b995-ad3a893cd8a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345623} {:answer/id #uuid "4a356632-3dd5-5ea1-a47b-554f60c49d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345987} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc38916-6632-5405-a6d9-6c55765a5bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346748} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ad93bf-d22c-545c-be65-567149ec4f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347352} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7019ea-e533-5abd-9898-ccdde8cd52da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f09dffb-bf3a-5fbb-b995-ad3a893cd8a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345623} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee16fde-27c3-566c-9103-dab0b178ad0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64e956c1-f323-5e83-8c0c-e6640c910184" :question/math-academy-id "q-79326" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w})=5$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
